{{!
	Notion skin: the icon primitive.

	A leaf partial - it includes no other partial and is the smallest unit of markup in
	the skin. Button.mustache, Link.mustache and Dropdown/Open.mustache each render it
	from inside an `icon` section, so the current context IS the icon name and this
	partial takes no named key of its own.

	string . the Codex/OOUI icon name to paint, for example `menu`, `search`,
		`userAvatar`, `appearance` or `listBullet`. A name paints a glyph only if some
		module has generated a matching mask rule for it; an unmatched name paints
		nothing at all, silently. This skin's own two packs are the usual providers -
		`skins.notion.icons` in skin.json, and `skins.notion.icons.js` for the icons
		only needed once the script module has loaded - but they are not the only ones.
		Extensions and gadgets ship their own icon modules against the same
		`mw-ui-icon-*` selectors, so a name absent from both of this skin's packs may
		still resolve where such a module is loaded: `wikimedia-bookmarkOutline`, which
		NotionComponentStickyHeader passes for the reading-lists control, comes from
		Extension:ReadingLists rather than from either pack here. Do not conclude from a
		name's absence in skin.json that it is dead. The value is emitted verbatim, so it may
		arrive already carrying the historical `wikimedia-` prefix that some components
		pass, and it may carry a variant suffix such as `-progressive` matching the
		pack's selectorWithVariant declaration. A falsy icon never reaches this partial
		- the calling section then renders nothing at all - which is how an icon-less
		button, link or dropdown label is expressed.

	The element is deliberately empty: the glyph is painted by the mask-image rule that
	OOUIIconPackModule generates for this skin's icon selector - the notion-icon class
	combined with the wikimedia-prefixed name class emitted below - which skin.json
	declares with useMaskImage true and useDataURI false. A masked glyph takes its
	colour from the element's own background-color, which is what lets every icon
	recolour straight from the Notion design-token layer instead of shipping one asset
	per colour. It is also deliberately unlabelled: at all three call sites the
	surrounding control already carries the accessible name, so a role or an aria-hidden
	attribute here would add noise without adding meaning.

	Of the four classes emitted below only the first, the skin's own hook, is renamed
	for this skin; it is what resources/skins.notion.styles/components/Icon.less and the
	generated icon-pack CSS are both written against. The other three are load-bearing
	platform contracts, reproduced byte for byte in their original order: core's plain
	icon hook, which is the class that matches when a component passes a name that
	already carries the prefix; the wikimedia-prefixed selector that OOUIIconPackModule
	actually generates, which if broken makes every icon in the skin silently go blank;
	and the Codex contract class that sizes and positions an icon inside a Codex button.

	No dimension, colour or inline style appears here: appearance is owned entirely by
	the stylesheet, which resolves Notion design tokens.
}}
<span class="notion-icon mw-ui-icon-{{.}} mw-ui-icon-wikimedia-{{.}} cdx-button__icon"></span>
